<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagat Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Shaheed-e-Azam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhagat Singh was an Indian anti-colonial revolutionary, born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1907, who became a martyr and folk hero in Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after his execution at the age of 23. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> Socialist Republican Association, in December 1928 he and Shivaram Rajguru shot dead the junior police officer John P. Saunders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, mistaking him for the superintendent James Scott, whom they held responsible for the death of Lala Lajpat Rai. In April 1929 he and Batukeshwar Dutt set off two low-intensity bombs in the Central Legislative Assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and let themselves be arrested, and his hunger strike in jail won wide sympathy. He was convicted of murder and hanged in March 1931.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh.jpg" alt="Bhagat Singh" width="330" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahore_conspiracy_case_poster_9th_Oct_193o_jindal_sunam_12x9_copy_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1928 the all-British Simon Commission was boycotted; Lala Lajpat Rai died weeks after being beaten in a police baton charge at Lahore, and <b>Bhagat Singh</b> and his comrades shot dead a young police officer in revenge.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also been associated with the younger generation of freedom fighters between the wars, among them Ashfaqullah Khan, <b>Bhagat Singh</b> and Chandrashekhar Azad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li></ul></section>
